<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karrani dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1564">1564</a>–<a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Karrani Afghans, Karranis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karrani dynasty was an Afghan dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from 1564 to 1576, the last independent sultans of Bengal. Taj Khan Karrani took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> in 1564, and his brother Sulaiman Khan Karrani moved the capital to Tanda, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> in 1568 and outwardly acknowledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. His son Daud Khan defied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and was defeated and killed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rajmahal">Battle of Rajmahal</a> in 1576.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karrani-dynasty.jpg" alt="Karrani dynasty" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manohar, 1590 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Da%27ud_at_the_Battle_of_Tukaroi_(3_March_1575).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The founder, Taj Khan Karrani, was an Afghan of the Pashtun Karlani tribe from Bangash, who had served the Sur emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>. Between 1562 and 1564 he captured south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, and by assassinating the last Muhammed Shahi ruler he seized all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, restoring the Bengal Sultanate under his own house. The capital was at first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>.</p><p>His brother Sulaiman Khan Karrani moved the seat of government in 1565 to Tanda, also in Malda, and in 1568 annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> to the sultanate for good. His prime minister Lodi Khan kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> content with gifts and banquets, and Sulaiman's authority reached from Koch Bihar to Puri and from the Son River to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a>, the last ruler of the house, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> attacked. The Mughal general Munim Khan took the capital Tanda on 25 September 1574, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tukaroi">Battle of Tukaroi</a> on 3 March 1575 forced Daud to withdraw to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>. By the Treaty of Katak that followed, Daud ceded the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and kept only Orissa.</p><p>The settlement broke down after Munim Khan died, aged 50, in October 1575. Daud invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and declared his independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. The Mughal campaign against the sultanate ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rajmahal">Battle of Rajmahal</a> on 12 July 1576, won by the Mughal general Khan Jahan I, after which Daud was executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Karrani house was the last independent dynasty to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> before it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subah">Subah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Their fall did not end resistance at once: Pashtun chiefs and the local landlords known as the Baro Bhuyans, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a>, fought on against the Mughals, and Bengal was firmly consolidated as a Mughal province only in 1612, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karrani dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Karrani Afghans</b>, who took Gaur in 1564 and were led from about 1566 by Sulaiman Khan Karrani, ruled from Gaur and later Tanda, conquered Orissa in 1568 and outwardly acknowledged Akbar to avoid war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He was defeated by the <b>Karrani Afghans</b> of Bengal in 1568, ending independent Odisha.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhoi-dynasty">Bhoi dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Karrani Afghans</b> overthrew independent Odisha in 1568, Ramachandra Deva I, of the Bhoi family, established himself at Khurda near Puri.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-khurda">Kingdom of Khurda</a></li></ul></section>
